gcm/live-draw: fragment WPOS counts pixels, not samples, on an antialiased target (LD_WPOS_AA=0 old).

Symptom (Killzone 2, Corinth River): once the level fades in, the left half of the 1280x720 picture holds the whole
scene squeezed 2x horizontally and the right half is white with horizontal streaks (edge at x = 640).

Cause: live-draw keeps an antialiased surface at its sample resolution (rsx_dispatch.h: DIAGONAL_CENTERED_2 = 2x1
samples per pixel, SQUARE_* = 2x2) and maps the guest's pixel viewport over it, so SV_Position, which the fragment
decompiler returns for WPOS, counts samples. The RSX runs the fragment program once per pixel and WPOS counts pixels.
Killzone 2 draws its G-buffer and the deferred lighting into 2560x720 sample surfaces (1280x720 pixels, 2x
horizontal: 0x7B0000 / 0x1E30000 / 0xF30000 / 0x16B0000, depth 0x25B0000); the lighting programs address the G-buffer
from WPOS, so the left 1280 samples of the light buffer 0x1E30000 read the whole G-buffer and the right 1280 read past
its edge (clamped). The resolve into the display buffer was right: it showed the light buffer as it was.

Fix: a fragment program built for an antialiased target reads WPOS as the centre of the pixel that holds the sample,
(floor(p.xy / aa) + 0.5, p.z, p.w) (text substitution of input.position after decompilation). The sample grid is
in the pipeline key only when it is not 1x1, so other pipelines keep their keys (warm caches stay warm). PSO template
records (patch 2071) carry it in flags bits 8-23 (RSX_PT_WPOS_AA_SHIFT), so the template prewarm builds the same key
(from Project Helghast; without it the prewarm's key check failed for every antialiased record).
ASSUMPTION: the guest does not shade per sample (NV47 runs one fragment per pixel).

Upstream candidate (general: any title that reads WPOS while rendering into a multisampled surface).
Verification: Killzone 2 under Proton.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (hand-merged): live-draw both sides kept

